# HQL-0062: `io.process`, signals, and the Unix process model

Companion to [[HQL-0062]]. A script is a Unix process. This note compares
what a Unix process can do with what an HQL program can do today, defines
the module `io.process` for the process's identity and its signals, and
names the module each remaining piece belongs to.

!!! warning "Design only"
    Nothing in this note is implemented, and [[HQL-0062]] does not deploy
    it. The declarations are a design direction, written under the rules of
    the companion note [pure and effectful](01-pure-and-effectful.hmd).

!!! abstract "TL;DR"
    - **`io.env` keeps the invocation**: `var`, `vars`, `arg`, `args` and
      `argv`, the values fixed before the program started.
    - **`io.process` is the running process**: its identifier, the signals
      it receives, as a `Stream<Signal>`, and the signals it sends, as an
      effectful operation that returns a `Result`.
    - **Missing in the process**: its identifier, signals in both
      directions, and child processes.
    - **Missing in IPC**: named pipes, Unix domain sockets, a listening
      socket, UDP and file locks. Each is an endpoint, in `io.fs` or
      `io.net`, not in `io.process`.
    - **Not wanted**: shared memory, semaphores and System V or POSIX
      message queues.

## The Unix process model, and what HQL has

| A Unix process | HQL today | Module |
|---|---|---|
| reads its arguments | `arg`, `args`, and `argv` with [[HQL-0062]] | `io.env` |
| reads its environment | `var`, `vars`, under the `env` grant | `io.env` |
| reads and writes its three standard streams | `stdin`, `stdout`, `stderr` | `io` |
| has a working directory | `working_dir()` | `io.fs` |
| reports an exit status | the program's result ([[HQL-0062]], the section "The exit status") | none: no operation sets it |
| has an identifier and a parent | missing | `io.process` |
| receives signals | missing: every signal has its default action | `io.process` |
| sends a signal to a process | missing | `io.process` |
| starts a child, writes its input, reads its output and status | missing | `io.process` |
| reads and writes a file | `file(path)`, `file(path).end` | `io` |
| reads and writes a named pipe | missing: `File` is `Replayable` and a pipe is not | `io.fs` |
| connects to a TCP address | `tcp(host, port)` | `io.net` |
| listens on a TCP address and accepts connections | missing | `io.net` |
| sends and receives UDP datagrams | missing | `io.net` |
| connects to and listens on a Unix domain socket | missing | `io.net` |
| locks a file against another process | missing | `io.fs` |
| shares memory, or a semaphore, with another process | not wanted | none |
| uses a System V or POSIX message queue | not wanted: `io.msg` holds the brokers | none |

An anonymous pipe needs no operation of its own. Between two processes the
shell makes it, and each side reads `stdin` or writes `stdout`:
`./a.hql | ./b.hql`. Inside one program it is what connects a child's
standard streams to its parent, so it arrives with child processes.

Shared memory and semaphores are refused because an HQL value is immutable
and an evaluation holds no state that two processes could write. Two
processes that share data pass it through an endpoint.

## `io.process`

`io.process` holds what is true of the process while it runs. `io.env`
holds what the invocation fixed before it started. A script that reads its
command line imports `io.env` alone.

```hql
module io.process

/// A signal, by its POSIX name without the `SIG` prefix: `Signal("TERM")`.
/// `number` is the number this platform gives it.
type Signal {
    name   : String
    number : Int
}

/// The signal named `name`. A name this platform does not define is an
/// unrecoverable failure that states the name.
fn Signal(name: String) : Signal

/// Why a signal was not sent: `reason` is `no-such-process`,
/// `not-permitted` or `unsupported`.
type SendFailure : DataContract {
    to     : Int
    signal : Signal
    reason : String
}

/// The identifier of this process.
effectful fn id() : Int

/// The identifier of the process that started this one.
effectful fn parent_id() : Int

/// The signals named, as they are delivered to this process, one element
/// for each delivery. The stream does not end.
effectful fn signals(...of: Signal) : Stream<Signal>

/// Sends `signal` to the process `to`.
effectful fn send(signal: Signal, *, to: Int) : Result<Step, SendFailure>
```

### Receiving a signal

A signal is an event from outside the evaluation, so it arrives as a
source: `signals` gives a `Stream<Signal>`.

```hql
#!/usr/bin/env hql
from io.process import *

// One line for each SIGHUP or SIGUSR1 the process receives.
signals(Signal("HUP"), Signal("USR1")) | map(s => s.name) | sink(stdout, Text)
```

- From the first call that names a signal, `hql` MUST deliver that signal
  to the stream and MUST NOT take its default action. A process that reads
  `signals(Signal("TERM"))` no longer ends on `SIGTERM`.
- A signal no call names MUST keep its default action.
- `signals(Signal("KILL"))` and `signals(Signal("STOP"))` MUST be refused,
  because no process receives either.
- Two deliveries of one signal that the kernel merges are one element. The
  stream MUST NOT be read as a count of the signals sent.
- Two calls that name the same signal MUST each receive every delivery.

### Sending a signal

Sending a signal is a write to another process, as a request to an HTTP
resource is a write to another host. It is an effect, it returns a value,
and its failure is a `Result`.

```hql
#!/usr/bin/env hql
from io.process import *
import io.env

// `./stop.hql 4242` exits 0 when the signal was sent, and 1 with the
// reason when it was not.
send(Signal("TERM"), to = to_int(env.argv()[0]))
```

- `send` MUST give `ok` when the kernel accepted the signal. That states
  delivery to the process and nothing about what the process did with it.
- `send` MUST give `err(SendFailure)` when no process has the identifier
  or this process may not signal it.
- `send` MUST NOT end this process when `to` is `id()` and the signal's
  default action is to end it, unless no call to `signals` names that
  signal. The signal is delivered as any other is.

### The grant

Both directions are controlled by one grant, a top-level key of `hql.toml`
beside `env` ([[HQL-0059]]):

```toml
process = ["receive", "send"]
```

- Absent, a run MUST grant both. Stated, it grants what it lists.
- `hql render` MUST grant neither. A rendered document does not own the
  process that renders it.
- `id()` and `parent_id()` MUST need no grant.
- A call the grant refuses is a forbidden effect, an unrecoverable failure
  ([[HQL-0033]]).

### Platforms

Signals are a Unix mechanism. On Windows `signals` MUST deliver `INT` for
Ctrl-C and `TERM` for a close request, and no other; `send` MUST give
`err` with the reason `unsupported`.

## What a signal handler needs that HQL lacks

A program that follows a file and stops cleanly on `SIGTERM` has two
streams: the lines and the signals. The first has to end when the second
gives its first element. `lib/std` declares `take_while`, which tests an
element of its own stream, and `merge` and `zip`, which join two streams
and end neither early.

What is missing is a way to run two pipelines at the same time and end
both when one ends. It is not a Unix pipe, and it reaches nothing outside
the evaluation. The companion note [`parallel`](06-parallel.hmd) defines
it as `parallel(join = any) { … }`, with this program as its example. A
program whose only stream is `signals`, as the example above, needs no
such construct.

!!! note "Two meanings of pipe"
    An HQL pipe, `a | b`, composes computation: the value of `a` is the
    input of the step `b` ([[pipes]]). A Unix pipe is a kernel object that
    carries bytes from one process to another. This note writes "pipeline
    step" for the first, and "anonymous pipe" or "named pipe" for the
    second.

## The remaining pieces

Each is an endpoint or an operation on one, so each belongs to the module
that owns the outside state it reaches, not to `io.process`.

| Piece | Shape | Module |
|---|---|---|
| a child process | an endpoint, `{Readable, Writable}`: a write goes to its standard input, a read comes from its standard output, and its exit status is a `Result` | `io.process` |
| a named pipe | an endpoint, `{Readable, Writable}` and not `Replayable`, beside `File` and `FileEnd`, defined in the companion note [named pipes](05-named-pipes.hmd) | `io.fs` |
| a Unix domain socket | an endpoint, `{Readable, Writable}`, beside `Tcp` | `io.net` |
| a listening socket | a `Stream` of connections, each an endpoint | `io.net` |
| UDP | an endpoint whose documents are datagrams | `io.net` |
| a file lock | a scope, as `with transaction` is | `io.fs` |

A child process is the largest of these. With it a script replaces a shell
pipeline, and without it a script is only ever one stage of one. The
companion note [child processes](03-child-processes.hmd) defines it.
